The int8 GEMM's kernel is a LOOP NEST, and reading it as one loop overstated
starvation by 1.8x -- supersedes results/aie/gemm_cost_model.log
2026-09-09, same day, same machine. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), mlir-aie ironenv
(v1.4.2, llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201). NO NPU SESSION was built and no hardware context was
taken; this reads two already-compiled kernel objects and computes. The measured microseconds
are quoted from results/aie/int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log (2026-09-07), not re-run.

Commands (from the repo root, in the ironenv):
  python tools/gemm_cost_model.py ~/.npu/cache/e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76/matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o \
      --tile 64,64,64 --measured-us 7458.1
  python tools/gemm_cost_model.py ~/.npu/cache/0816364bbbaf03f83e2f0bcd/matmul_i8_i32_170456c5.o \
      --tile 64,64,32 --measured-us 14394.5

WHAT WAS WRONG. results/aie/gemm_cost_model.log, committed hours earlier, modelled
matmul_i8_i32 as ONE hardware loop with straight-line setup around it, and charged the 135
non-loop bundles once per kernel call. That is not the shape of the function. It is a nest:
two software loops around the hardware loop, with the accumulators loaded before the hardware
loop and stored after it, and that whole body re-run once per group of live accumulators. The
error inflated the missing time and produced "the core is starved for 60% of the dispatch".
The corrected figure is 25.4%, and the dominant cost moves from data delivery to accumulator
traffic INSIDE the core. Both logs are kept; this one supersedes the other's numbers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE EVIDENCE THAT IT IS A NEST
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Three things in the disassembly, none of which the first reading accounted for.

(a) Two backward branches AFTER the hardware loop ends, each preceded by its own induction
    variable update and bound test:

     328: 7b 0b 5a 03 40 81 06 55 58 ff	vlda	amhl0, [sp, #-0x160];		vst	amlh0, [p5, #0x20];		add	r13, r13, #0x2
     332: bd 7c 5c 0b b0 a0    	vst	amll0, [p5], #0x40;		ltu	r14, r13, r7
     338: 3b 61 00 10 00 00 1c 68 d0 a0	vst	amhh0, [p5, #0x20];		jnz	r14, #0x0
     342: 59 82 05 0d  	vst	amhl0, [p5], #0x40
     346: 59 81 06 0d  	vst	amlh0, [p5, #0x20]
     34a: 59 80 05 0d  	vst	amll0, [p5], #0x40
     34e: 59 83 06 0d  	vst	amhh0, [p5, #0x20]
     352: bd 50 18 4b b0 a0    	vst	amhl0, [p5], #0x40;		add	r12, r12, r5
     358: 19 13 94 12  	add	r10, r10, #0x4
     35c: 99 8c 98 12  	ltu	r12, r10, r8
     360: 95 01 40 00 00 60    	jnz	r12, #0x0
    add r13, r13, #0x2 / ltu r14, r13, r7 / jnz r14   -- the inner software loop
    add r10, r10, #0x4 / ltu r12, r10, r8 / jnz r12   -- the outer software loop

(b) The hardware loop body issues eight vmac into cm0..cm7 and contains NO accumulator
    store. The stores are the `vst amll*/amlh*/amhl*/amhh*` bundles above, outside it, so
    the eight accumulators must be dumped and the loop re-entered for the next group.

(c) The loop bounds are compile-time constants in the function entry, so the trip counts are
    readable rather than assumed. In the n=64 object: `mova r3, #0x8` feeds
    `add.nc lc, r3, #-0x2`, so the hardware loop runs 6 times; `mova r7, #0x6` with r13
    starting at -2 and stepping 2 gives 4 inner iterations; `mov r8, #0xc` with r10 starting
    at -4 and stepping 4 gives 4 outer iterations. 4 x 4 = 16 accumulator groups.

That reconciles exactly against the work. A 4x2 grid of accumulators, each a 4x8 block, is a
16x16 output region; a 64x64 output tile holds 16 of them, and m/16 = 4 outer by n/16 = 4
inner. Each group reduces the whole k extent: 8 accumulators x (k/s) = 8 x 8 = 64 vmac, of
which the software pipeline peels 2 k-steps (16 vmac), leaving 6 hardware-loop iterations.
16 groups x 64 vmac = 1,024, which is exactly (64x64x64)/(4x8x8). Nothing is left over.

