--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  tile          vmac      issuing cycles   measured    issuing   MAC rate over
                per call     per call     cycles/call  fraction   the whole call
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  m64 k64 n64      1024          2442          3274      74.6%   107.3 (41.9% of 256)
  m64 k64 n32       512          1306          3160      41.3%   100.4 (39.2% of 256)

  superseded (one-loop reading):   1323 / 40.4% / 198.1 and 738 / 23.4% / 177.5

FIRST, THE SCHEDULE IS THE LARGER LOSS, AND THE FIRST READING PUT IT IN THE WRONG PLACE.
Over a whole call this kernel issues about 100-107 MACs per cycle against the 256 the tile
can retire, roughly 40% -- not the 77.4% the one-loop reading gave. The 88.9% figure for the
inner loop is correct and unchanged, but it covers only 54 of the 141 cycles an accumulator
group costs. The other 87 bundles per group are accumulator loads, accumulator stores and
stack spill traffic, run 16 times per call at n=64. That is a direct consequence of a number
this branch already measured: five live 4x8x8 int8 accumulators is the spill-free ceiling
(results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log) and this kernel holds eight, with a 416-byte frame.

SECOND, THE PER-BUFFER FLOOR IS REAL AND IS THE FINDING THAT SURVIVED.
Measured cycles per call are 3,274 at n=64 and 3,160 at n=32 -- a 3.6% difference for buffers
whose compute differs by 2x. That is a measurement, not a model output, and neither reading
changes it. What the corrected model changes is how full that slot is: n=32 puts 1,306
issuing cycles into a ~3,200-cycle slot and n=64 puts 2,442 into it. So n=64 is 1.93x faster
because it nearly fills a slot whose length barely moves, and the remaining headroom is about
1.3x, not the 2.4x the first reading claimed.

THIRD, A CANDIDATE CONSTANT IS NOW FALSIFIED. The first log bounded the handoff by charging
the trace probe's entire measured 717 cycles per buffer. At the corrected issuing cost that
bound predicts 117.5% of the measured time at n=64, which is impossible. So the probe's 717
does NOT transfer to another kernel, exactly as its decomposition said it should not: 512 of
it was that probe's own trace-flush loop and 190 its kernel prologue, and only the ~15-cycle
acquire/release is a property of the ObjectFifo.

WHAT IS STILL UNEXPLAINED, AND THE TEST FOR IT
832 cycles per call at n=64, 1,854 at n=32 -- the difference between the issuing cost and the
measured time. Bytes into one core's L1 are 8,192 and 6,144 per call, so the achieved input
rate is 2.50 and 1.94 bytes per cycle against the 3.35 and 4.71 a never-starved core would
need. Both hypotheses from the first log survive with corrected magnitudes:

  H9.  Stream-port tracing measures a sustained input rate at or below 2.5 B/cycle into a
       core. Fails if the ports read faster, which would move the missing time elsewhere.
  H10. Per-buffer wall time is a floor set by the data path, so throughput rises with work
       per buffer until the issuing cost approaches ~3,200 cycles. Headroom at n=64 is ~1.3x,
       not 2.4x. Fails if a larger tile does not raise throughput.

  H11. NEW, and the one this correction opens. The 87 non-loop bundles per accumulator group
       are spill traffic caused by holding eight accumulators where five is the spill-free
       ceiling. A variant built to hold four or five accumulators per group would trade more
       groups against a shorter body. It is testable entirely statically -- rebuild mm.cc with
       a different accumulator blocking and re-run this tool -- and predicts a shorter inner
       body per vmac. Fails if the per-group bundle count does not fall, which would mean the
       87 bundles are the C tile's mandatory load/store rather than spill.

H10 has a known obstacle already on record: int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log's PROBE A (m=128,
n=64) and PROBE B (k=128, n=64) both failed to build with "'aie.tile' op Basic sequential
allocation failed", an L1 capacity limit rather than a performance result.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Nothing was measured on hardware in this run. The issuing-cycle figures are computed from
  object code; the microseconds they are compared against come from a run two days earlier.
- "Not issuing" is a residual, not an observation. It is consistent with lock stall, the only
  stall category results/aie/pmu_probe_npu.log has yet seen non-zero, but nothing here
  attributes it. A port or stall trace on this design is what would.
- The nest walk assumes the two backward branches target the two .LBB labels in order. The
  branch targets are unresolved relocations in a .o file and were not read directly; the
  reconciliation of trip counts against the required vmac count is the evidence for the
  reading, and the tool refuses to produce a number when that reconciliation fails.
- The 15-cycle acquire/release constant was measured on a one-core, one-fifo probe with a
  different kernel. Its transfer to a two-fifo GEMM is still an assumption; what this log
  adds is that the opposite extreme, 717 per fifo, is now ruled out.
- One power mode (Default, 1.7983 GHz), one dtype, one design. The conv kernels are untouched.
- The correction to aie2_isa_static.log section 5's PROVENANCE, recorded in
  results/aie/gemm_cost_model.log section 0, stands unchanged: the object disassembled there
  is the n=32 build, not the n=64 one behind 4607.05 GOPS, and the two loops are identical.
